tasks\results\ATLAS-H1-CORE-HARDENING.progress.md-101-- **Lane F accepted (channels production), committed `113cf1e`.** GAP-1: `handle_webhook` and the supervision outbound path now fall back to the credential store when `configs["secret"]` is absent (explicit config still wins; store errors are logged, not swallowed). GAP-2: `configure()` diff-detects connection-affecting changes and a single coalescing reconciler restarts only the affected channel's supervisor (deferred-startup safe via `_started` flag; no-op writes trigger nothing). Six new regression tests in `test_channels_manager.py`; focused **26 passed (2.84s)**, adjacent channels/gateway **87 passed (5.50s)**, server-touching suites **31 passed (84.10s)**, ruff clean, no lingering processes.
tasks\results\ATLAS-H1-CORE-HARDENING.progress.md-102-- **Z1 speed ammunition (controller, from `artifacts/h1r-r6-diagnostic/unit.xml`):** slowest unit-layer cases: `test_daily_browser_multistep` 96.2s, `TestWatcher::test_local_git_backfills_large_gap` 75.8s, `test_capability_bus_can_disable_and_reenable_optional_integrations` 75.6s, `test_daily_edit_diff_review_then_fix` 57.3s, plus a k1_daily_paths cluster of 20–28s cases — prime wrong-layer candidates for the unit→integration re-marking plus xdist evaluation.
tasks\results\ATLAS-H1-CORE-HARDENING.progress.md-103-- **Lane C accepted (R7.0 usage aggregation closeout, Z2), Atlas side committed `b872092` (hermes_runtime.py on_usage filter deferred until Lane D drains to avoid capturing mid-flight edits).** Aggregation pipeline is complete end to end: fork emits one usage row per provider response (`{turn_id}:api:{n}` request ids, purpose conversation/title/compression/subagent, cache buckets + `cache_usage_fields` names-only), subagent rows join the parent queue exactly once and dedupe downstream by request_id; Atlas budget gets reserve/record/release/**refund** semantics (rows with `request_sent: false` refund silently and never reach the audit stream — closes two real WIP-halfway bugs: title requests rejected before sending were charged as unknown-usage audit rows, and budget mode still took a hidden primary-transport retry). Outlets: CLI `atlas run` summary prints tokens/requests/per-purpose across all output formats; server `record_usage` dedupes into run.usage; audit file is a separate stream. Contract e2e `test_run_usage_aggregation_e2e.py` **passes** (4 main + 1 subagent, purpose_totals, 5 audit rows); the 09:28 hang did not reproduce (generic post-summary lingering, solo run exits clean). Focused selection (11 files + contract): **95 passed, 500.72s, exit 0, clean process exit**; before: 8 failed/85 passed. Fork closeout: branch `h1r2-usage` commit `d85c8ae62d5a80ac09793f403db5b58f544a5313` pushed to `unicorn-Y/hermes-agent`; Atlas pin + VENDORED updated; fork-side selections **327 + 312 passed**. **Cache=0 root cause (R7.0 final item):** the relay DOES return cache fields — real smoke-run engine logs show 86–99% cache hits from each scenario's 2nd request (proof the request prefix is byte-stable; the stable-prefix contract test also passes byte-exact). The old Atlas pipeline dropped the fork-resolved cache buckets, so summaries showed 0. `normalize_usage` now reads the four candidate fields (first non-zero wins); R7.1 rerun will pin the exact relay field name (most likely `prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens`). No prefix fix needed.
tasks\results\ATLAS-H1-CORE-HARDENING.progress.md:104:- **Lane G accepted (CLI post-completion hang), committed `e39d341`.** Reproduced at zero model cost with a FakeLLMServer-backed subprocess harness + `faulthandler.dump_traceback_later`: ordinary runs exit in 2.02s (in-process) / 0.03s (`--server`); the hang is **plan-mode specific** — `_drive_turn` had no terminal handling for `run.plan.proposed`, so a headless run parked in `ws.recv()` until `--timeout` (all 52 remaining threads are daemon; the controller's thread-leak hypothesis is disproven; scenario 08's exit 124 was the harness clock beating the CLI's own 600s timeout). Fix (controller-approved behavior decision): non-interactive `run.plan.proposed` → treated as denied → `run.cancel` → `run.cancelled` → **exit 3** with `summary.plan_denied=true`, matching H1 §11's "需要审批但非交互 = 拒绝" contract; the server-side plan-approval flow is untouched (WS-level plan tests still pass). Three new subprocess regression tests (`test_cli_headless.py`, integration marker): exit within 30s of completion in in-process and `--server` modes, and plan-mode deterministic exit 3 with `plan_denied`. Whole `test_cli_headless.py` **16 passed**; focused trio **3 passed, 49.1s**; all pytest processes exited cleanly. Consequence: the old live `test_full_08` design (CLI parks awaiting a second-WS approval) is obsolete by design — rewrite dispatched as Lane H (WS-driven plan→approve→execute).
tasks\results\ATLAS-H1-CORE-HARDENING.progress.md-105-- **Lane H accepted (live scenario 08 rewrite), committed `0f91e4c`.** `test_full_08_plan_propose_approve_then_execute` now drives a single `/ws/chat` connection end to end: `run.start` (plan mode, task.md verbatim) → `run.plan.proposed` (420s budget) → assert planning executed nothing (`greet.py` absent, verbatim from the old test) → `run.plan.approve` on the same socket → terminal `run.completed` with result=completed (600s) → workspace artifact + scenario `check.py`. New WS-native assertions strengthen it: `data.phase=="planning"` on `run.started`, non-empty plan goal/subtasks, at least one `tool.started` after the approval, real usage tokens, `changes.files >= 1`. Evidence discipline: all envelopes to `stream.jsonl` (redacted), harness timing notes to `stderr.log`, `meta.json` records `driven_via`, plan/event ids and per-stage timestamps; partial evidence is written before any assert and on any BaseException. Zero shared-helper changes (regressions untouched, still 8 passed twice); collect-only clean (8/12, 4 live deselected); ruff clean on new code; no lingering processes. Known trade-off recorded: WS version drops the CLI exit-code signal (covered instead by Lane G's plan-mode regression); approval latency is not asserted (real desktop approvals are sub-second per old live evidence).
tasks\results\ATLAS-H1-CORE-HARDENING.progress.md-106-- **Lane I accepted (layering + gate tooling), committed `bb3e892`.** Key finding: five of the six slow-test candidates were already moved to integration by `bf42417` (module/class lists in `core/tests/conftest.py`); the 365s diagnostic itself ran under the old script's hard-coded forced xdist. The lane verified each existing migration is honest (real uvicorn subprocess + Playwright Chromium; real git with 110–120 commit fixtures; real LSP subprocess), moved the one remaining offender (`test_p18x_integrations.py::test_capability_bus_can_disable_and_reenable_optional_integrations` — real CLI `--version` and `codex login status` probes outside patch context) with a bare `@pytest.mark.integration`, and left an honest "slow but stays unit" list (concurrency_isolation 16.8s fully monkeypatched, runtime_resources, c1_lifecycle_subtask in-process, etc.). `run_h1r_r6_gates.ps1` gained `-Parallel [int]` (xdist probe, auto-fallback to serial, parallel-named evidence files, serial default preserved; desktop/frontend always serial). Collection identity holds: **2278 = 2013 unit + 249 integration + 1 desktop + 15 live**. pytest-xdist 3.8.0 already present in the venv. EXECUTION-GUIDE rewritten with lane commands and budgets; controller owns the timing runs. **A14 acceptance mode set by controller: `-Parallel` timing runs (taskbook explicitly allows xdist given 3 consecutive green runs).**
tasks\results\ATLAS-H1-CORE-HARDENING.progress.md:107:- **Lane D accepted (runtime), committed `7efd21e` (its two test files; the hermes_runtime.py +8 is Lane C's deferred on_usage refund filter, attribution confirmed).** 8 of 11 scope items did not reproduce (green in isolation AND in an 18-file pollution prefix — consistent with the takeover-run pollution mechanism); `test_runtime_bridges_thread_callbacks`, 20-wave concurrency (3 full runs), c1_subtask, c4_hooks, fork_persists, wave3 both — all green untouched. wave2 ephemeral fixed by patching the whole services seam (the wave4 TestClient leaves CLOSED components in `runtime_mgr.services`, which hijacks later tests). **Two real H2-era production regressions isolated (verified with minimal fixes, then reverted per scope — dispatched as Lane J):** (1) `rewind_conversation` does `json.loads(item.get("metadata"))` but metadata is now a dict → TypeError swallowed → run_ids always empty → **checkpoint file restore silently dead since H2** (messages rewind fine, files don't); (2) `fork_conversation`'s summarize branch was mechanically switched from `save_message_first` to `append_session_message` → **fork summary now lands at transcript tail instead of head**. Stability note for A6: 3×20-wave green; the intermittent post-summary process lingering persists under load (faulthandler `dump_traceback_later(600, exit=True)` wrapper proven as a hard-exit guard for gate runs — controller will use it). D's final 7-file state: 2 failed / 91 passed, both failures being exactly the two api/ bugs above.
